// The two **misdiagnoses** that closed with the operand unification, pinned as the diagnostic they
// should always have been.
//
// A type parameter no channel reaches is E0058 — the code for a well-formed turbofish that cannot
// apply — with a message that names the missing channel. That is what `field_specs_of::<T>()` has
// reported since the turbofish arm learned to forward, and these two now report it through the same
// helper instead of each guessing:
//
//   * `roles_of::<E>()` said *"requires a `@semantic` enum, but `E` is not one"*. `E` may well be a
//     `@semantic` enum at every call site; the gate simply had nothing to gate, because the operand
//     was a parameter and not a type. The gate now applies to the static arm alone.
//   * `attributes_of::<T>()` said *"cannot forward `T` here: call-site-typed forwarding carries a
//     generic `fn`'s or method's OWN type parameters"* — which was true of the SLOT channel it read
//     and irrelevant to the receiver channel it could not, so it fired for a `T` that was reachable.
//
// Both survivors here are bodies a per-call channel genuinely cannot reach: a nested `fn` inside an
// instance method (the class's `T` rides the receiver's tag, and a nested `fn` has no receiver), and
// a nested `fn`'s OWN parameter (no call site fills a slot for it).
// expect: error E0058 at 37:50
// expect: error E0058 at 45:44
// expect: exit 1
@semantic
enum Kind {
    One
    Two
}

@attribute(Function)
struct Route { path: string }

class Scanner<T> {
    pub label: string

    fn new(label: string): Scanner<T> { return Scanner { label: label }; }

    fn nested(): int {
        fn inner(): int { return attributes_of::<T>().len() }
        if self.label == "" { return 0 }
        return inner();
    }
}

fn outer<E>(): int {
    // The enclosing `E` forwards; this nested declaration's own `F` has no call site to fill it.
    fn inner<F>(): int { return roles_of::<F>().len() }
    return inner() + roles_of::<E>().len();
}

s: Scanner<Route> = Scanner.new("routes");
echo s.nested();
echo outer::<Kind>();
